  "query_preview": "..\n\nBroad mid-upper trough centered over the eastern Great Lakes remains \nthe primary driver of local sensible weather through tonight. Ample \nlake moisture flux within northwesterly flow maintains robust \ncoverage of low cloud this evening through tonight. While embedded \nripples of vorticity rounding the trough could squeeze low level \nmoisture fields just enough for a couple light showers/drizzle this \nevening, vast majority stay dry for the rest of the day. These \nlocalized vort maxes are expected to reinvigorate ongoing lake \neffect showers along the southern basin of Lake Huron late evening-\ntonight however. While initially (through this evening) the eastern \nThumb would reside along their westernmost fringe, there is a window \novernight into early Sunday morning where lower level winds develop \na more northerly component, tied to reorganization of the upper \ntrough over the St Lawrence Seaway, allowing activity to push deeper \ninto the Thumb. Precip remains all rain for the lakeshore areas \ngiven the relatively mild Lake Huron. In the event showers shift \nfurther inland tonight, even these areas are favored to stay mostly \nor all rain as column saturation is confined sub 6kft well below the \nDGZ hampering ice nuclei formation. \n\nSunday is the transition day between departing eastern CONUS \ntroughing and approaching central CONUS ridging. Lingering lake \neffect showers along southern Lake Huron gradually wind down over \nthe course of the day as increasing subsidence and weak warm \nadvection from Midwestern surface high pressure work into SE MI. \nWeakening winds slow the pace lingering low level moisture is \nscoured out though, resulting in persistent cloudier skies and highs \nstill capped in the 40s.\n\nFirst half of the new work week is governed by ridging which is \nfirmly in place across the Great Lakes by this point. Steady \nmoderation in temperatures each day can be expected as above normal \nhighs in the 50s return. Influence of high pressure also leads to \nmostly sunny skies and modest southwesterly winds setting up a \npleasant stretch of weather. Next chances for rain don't arrive \nuntil late week as a Desert Southwest shortwave eventually reaches \nthe northern Great Lakes. That said, how much if any rain SE MI \ncould see is tied to how far north the wave tracks.",
